course: dictionary
lesson: 1209
title: Buddy: a friend and a partner
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: «Buddy» is an informal word. In «pay you back next week, buddy», it is a friendly way of talking to a friend, a bit like «mate». Context tells you the feeling is friendly.
explain: Look at «Children who leave their group on trips get lost quickly in crowds, so you stay with your group and your buddy.» Here a buddy is a partner you stay with, so you can look out for each other. The words «so» and «lost» are the clues.
explain: Buddy can also be a verb. «New pupils often feel invisible at first» and then «clubs, buddies and saying hi speed it up.» To buddy up means to pair up with someone as partners, so you are not on your own.
fact: A buddy is a friend or a partner.
fact: A buddy system means each child keeps an eye on a partner, so nobody gets lost.
fact: A buddy bench in the playground is where children sit if they want someone to play with.
fact: To buddy up means to pair up with someone as partners.
fact: Buddy is mostly used in American and Australian English; British English often says «mate» or «friend».
exercise: «Pay you back next week, buddy.» What does buddy mean here? | A bank that lends money | A teacher | *A friend | A stranger
exercise: On a school trip, what does a buddy system ask you to do? | Eat your lunch alone at the back | *Keep an eye on a partner so nobody gets lost | Count the buses before you leave | Walk alone at the front of the group
exercise: «Nadia wants to buddy up with someone for the swimming trip.» What does buddy up mean? | Fall asleep on the bus | Run away from the group | Ask a teacher for a sweet | *Pair up with someone as partners
test: «Teachers often notice lonely pupils and can help with clubs and buddies.» What is a buddy in this sentence? | A lonely pupil who sits alone | A school bus driver | *A friend or partner who helps you feel included | A kind of club rule
test: A reading buddy is... | A teacher who marks spellings | A book that is very long | A quiet corner of the library | *An older pupil who reads with a younger one
exam: Which sentence uses buddy as a verb? | *Mia and Sam buddied up for the science project | The buddy bench is painted bright red | Buddies are welcome at the club on Friday | Mia's buddy lives next door
